{"summary":"Source ledger: every claim limitation, every numbered part, and every specific structure, relationship, result, operating condition, and variant in the description and figure briefs traces to an affirmative passage in input/disclosure.md (identical text is repeated in the single USER turn of input/conversation.md). Spot checks of the load-bearing items: "first frame half 10 and second frame half 12 ... semicircular ... joined at one end ... by a hinge 14 ... releasably joined at the other end ... by a releasable latch 16" ← "a first semicircular frame half and a second semicircular frame half joined by a hinge at one end and a releasable latch at the other end"; "three jaw carriages 30 ... radial guide 38 ... jaw pad 40 ... pivotally mounted ... concave bearing face" ← "Three radial jaw carriages are distributed around the closed frame. Each carriage slides in a radial guide and carries a pivoting concave jaw pad ... without requiring a tool-specific pipe diameter"; "segmented cam ring 20 ... annular guide 18 ... first cam ring segment 22 ... second cam ring segment 24 ... complementary coupling faces 26/28 ... rotate together ... as a coupled ring" ← "A segmented cam ring is retained in an annular guide of the frame. One cam-ring segment is carried by each frame half. When the halves close, complementary drive faces at the hinge and latch ends couple the segments so that they move as one ring"; "follower 32 ... oblique slot 34 ... advance ... inwardly in synchronism" and "wedge 52 ... ring drive face 36 ... stroke" ← "Each jaw carriage has a follower received in a respective oblique slot ... Closing the latch drives a wedge against a drive face of the cam ring and rotates the coupled ring through a limited stroke. The oblique slots translate that rotation into synchronized inward travel of all three jaw carriages"; "carriage return spring 44" ← "A spring biases each carriage outward when the latch is released"; latch first/over-center positions, "force-limiting spring stack 54", "indicator pin 56 ... fixed edge of the latch housing 58 ... predetermined compression range ... not a measurement of that diameter", the install/remove method of claims 13-15, and "mounting boss 70 ... sensor, a tool, or a conduit ... not participating in the advance" each map to the corresponding sentences of paragraphs 9, 11 and 13 of the disclosure. Claims 1-15 add no limitation beyond that ledger; claim 10's assembly (clamp + boss + equipment) rests on "The frame may carry a mounting boss outside the annular guide for supporting a sensor, tool, or conduit." Background prior-art characterizations were not traced to the inventor, per instruction. Formalities match input/brief.md: title as given, no priority claim, no federally sponsored research, applicant/inventor Nimo Rotem.

Internal consistency: reference numerals 10-100 each carry exactly one meaning across numerals.md, the description, the claims and the figure briefs; no numeral appears in the text that is absent from the table, and every tabled numeral appears in at least one figure brief. Claim and description terminology agree ("coupled ring", "ring drive face", "force-limiting spring stack", "advance ... inwardly in synchronism"). The figure geometry is mutually consistent: FIG. 1's cutting-plane arrows put the FIG. 1 viewing side at the left of FIG. 3, which agrees with FIG. 2's slots opening toward the viewer; FIG. 4's reversed viewpoint correctly puts the second cam ring segment 24 (which carries the ring drive face 36 in FIG. 2) on the left, and the ramp handedness in FIG. 2, the drive direction in FIG. 4, and the slot handedness together produce inward jaw travel rather than retraction; FIG. 6's upper/lower assignment of frame halves matches FIG. 1. Renderer-choice items (hinge and latch outlines, carriage positions, pad and handle pivots, joint-face form, boss and equipment outlines, slot depth, guide opening direction) are hedged as "shown schematically" inside the figure briefs only; no "by way of example" or "any closed outline" broadening appears in the application text. No drafting note, placeholder, open question, unresolved alternative or internal comment remains in any file.

Two minor items only, both confined to figure briefs and drawing-reference wording: (1) the FIG. 3 section is indicated in FIG. 1 by a cutting-plane line and arrows with no view-number designation at its ends, contrary to the practice of 37 CFR 1.84(h)(3); (2) FIG. 4 asserts the direction of wedge travel ("downward movement drives the band left") without that choice being included in the brief's own schematic-depiction list, while the source is silent on the direction in which the wedge advances. Neither affects the claims, which remain neutral on both points.","findings":[{"severity":"minor","category":"formalities","title":"FIG. 3 section is indicated without the view-number designation on the cutting-plane line","where":"figures/FIG-1.md line 19; draft/07-drawings.md line 5; draft/08-detailed-description.md paragraph beginning "FIG. 3 is a fragmentary sectional view"","detail":"The cutting-plane line specified in the FIG. 1 brief carries direction arrows but no Arabic numeral at its ends identifying the sectional view, and the Brief Description and the detailed description both work around the missing designation by referring to "the plane indicated by the cutting-plane line and arrows in FIG. 1" instead of the conventional "line 3-3". 37 CFR 1.84(h)(3) contemplates that the ends of the broken line be designated by numerals corresponding to the view number of the sectional view. This is a drawing-formalities gap only; the section plane itself is fully described and is consistent between FIG. 1 and FIG. 3.","evidence":"figures/FIG-1.md: "A short broken cutting-plane line lies wholly in the upper part of the view, on the radial centre line of the topmost jaw carriage 30. ... A short arrow at each end points horizontally to the left of the view, both arrows to the same side of the line. That line is the cutting plane of FIG. 3, the arrows giving the direction in which that section is viewed." draft/07-drawings.md: "FIG. 3 is an enlarged fragmentary sectional view through one jaw carriage of the clamp of FIG. 1, taken on the plane indicated by the cutting-plane line and arrows in FIG. 1 ..." draft/08-detailed-description.md: "FIG. 3 is a fragmentary sectional view through one jaw carriage 30, taken on the plane indicated by the cutting-plane line and arrows in FIG. 1."","fix":"In figures/FIG-1.md, after the sentence describing the arrows, add: "The numeral 3 appears as drawing text at each end of the cutting-plane line, placed just outside that end alongside the arrow and clear of the frame body, the jaw pad 40 and the pipe 90." In draft/07-drawings.md line 5, replace "taken on the plane indicated by the cutting-plane line and arrows in FIG. 1, which plane contains" with "taken on line 3-3 of FIG. 1, which plane contains". In draft/08-detailed-description.md, replace "taken on the plane indicated by the cutting-plane line and arrows in FIG. 1." with "taken on line 3-3 of FIG. 1.""},{"severity":"minor","category":"figures_and_numerals","title":"FIG. 4 brief fixes the direction of wedge advance without including that choice in its schematic-depiction list","where":"figures/FIG-4.md line 3 (schematic list) and line 11 ("so downward movement drives the band left")","detail":"The FIG. 4 brief states that the wedge 52 advances radially inward (downward in the view) and thereby drives the cam ring circumferentially to the left. The disclosure says only that the latch "drives a wedge against a drive face of the cam ring and rotates the coupled ring through a limited stroke"; it never states the direction in which the wedge advances, and the FIG. 2 brief already treats the related choice - that the ring drive face lies on the outer boundary - as schematic. The direction of wedge travel is a kinematic relationship, so it must be carried as a depiction convention rather than as an assertion of disclosed structure. The brief's own schematic list omits it, while listing the housing form, handle attitude, handle outline, pivot mounting, frame-wall interruption, drive-face length, gap width and spring-stack symbol. The application text is already neutral on this point ("The inclined face of the wedge 52 converts that advance into circumferential movement of the segmented cam ring 20"), so the fix is confined to the figure brief.","evidence":"figures/FIG-4.md line 3: "Shown schematically: the form of the housing and cavity, the attitude of the latch handle 50 in the over-center position, the frame-wall interruption, the length of the ring drive face 36, the width of the gap between the two frame bodies, the pivot mounting of the latch handle 50 and the opening it passes through, the outline of that handle, and the force-limiting spring stack 54 drawn as one spring symbol." figures/FIG-4.md line 11: "A block, the wedge 52, lies in the cavity; its lower left face slants at the same angle as the ring drive face 36 and bears on it, so downward movement drives the band left." Nearest source passage, which shows the gap: input/disclosure.md: "Closing the latch drives a wedge against a drive face of the cam ring and rotates the coupled ring through a limited stroke."","fix":"In figures/FIG-4.md line 3, extend the schematic list to read: "... the outline of that handle, the direction in which the wedge 52 advances against the ring drive face 36 and the resulting sense of movement of the segmented cam ring 20, and the force-limiting spring stack 54 drawn as one spring symbol." Leave line 11 and all application text unchanged."}]}
